 it down to the day to  day wash your hands  don't go near Pete other people for  teenagers I think they felt quite a bit  of existential angst around it but  because it's not hitting teenagers as  much as other age groups I think they're  feeling pretty protected from it in fact  so the biggest threat is changes to  families in terms of grandparents and  elderly relatives and vulnerable  relatives so a lot of young people are  dealing with that threat okay actually  and and more what they're experiencing  difficulties with this is a day-to-day  I'm not being able to see their friends  what advice would you give to parents as  a child psychologist to help them  support their children in coping with  this crisis it's a really difficult time  and parents are going to be putting a  lot of expectations on themselves and  actually it's the toughest job in the  world at the best of times so right now  is gonna be very very tough so as a  parent just set yourself small goals  what is it that you would like to do to  make sure that that day's gone okay a  couple of things that you want to get  done  go very easy on yourself as a parent but  in terms of your child don't worry about  them having to you know be incredibly  educated during this time you just need  to tread water with the learning a lot  of parents are very anxious about screen  time and how much time their children  especially teenagers are spending gaming  during the day and again it's just about  having to take just take a step back as  a parent and think what is it that I  need to do right now I need to keep my  child well and safe and healthy and  these just involve little steps they  don't need to involve any big changes  thank you so much that was an herit  Rudkin a she's a child psychologist at  the University of Southampton thanks for  talking with us well the coronavirus  lockdown has sometimes meant loneliness  especially for the elderly here among  the most vulnerable in this pandemic  they're cut off from friends and  relatives they haven't seen for months  but one enterprising belgian businessman  has come up with an idea that's taken  some of the distance out of social  distancing Clementine has been waiting  for this moment for almost two months  her husband is in this Brussels care  home the last time she saw him was in  early March now they're reunited for a  few precious minutes this is great isn't  it it's great I think I'll have to stay  here the idea of using cranes to hold  people closer together was a brainchild  of Tristan fundin Bosch he's a manager  of the company that rents out the  platforms for window cleaning and fruit  picking  exactly month I saw somebody waving at  his mother two or three floors up and I  thought given that we have a serious  fall in business we can lift that man up  to the window with our machines and  that's what we did  84 year old Clementine says that the  crane ride has given her at least a  semblance of intimacy with her loved one  I am very happy that I could speak to  him that closely it's great I really  appreciate this initiative now  clementines back down on solid ground  and her husband's still in lockdown even  so just a few minutes with him have made  a huge difference  [Applause]
 

